 and tries to find just the  right thing to end the week so that that  is how it works it's still old-fashioned  journalism yes yeah it's not people  sending us ideas generally no that's  very interesting i would have thought  that that would have been so much easier  when charles corral did this job you  know 50 years ago when he started people  would send in things to cbs because it  was just cbs nbc and abc and everybody  watched charles kuralt and he would have  a folder if he's going to poughkeepsie  he would have like a folder of 100 story  ideas he could do in poughkeepsie it's  not that way anymore it's a little we  have to sort of work a little harder to  find our ideas  speaking of working a little bit harder  during the pandemic we all worked a lot  harder things changed so much for us for  you as you storytell around america but  you're not meeting these folks face to  face and you're doing it virtually what  was that like how did that change your  perspective it was really hard you know  i do on the road and all of a sudden i'm  in my house you know it's not the nature  of the segment we did the best we could  over zoom i incorporated my children  and did some things with them because  i'm stuck at home and they're in my  office hounding me anyway might as well  put them on tv so we did some of that  but i'm glad that it's over because  something's lost when you can't go out  and actually meet people face to face  and we're going places that cbs news  doesn't get to as often as sometimes  it's the back roads it's the smaller  towns and i think it's important to get  out there and represent those folks too  it is important to to represent all of  america  and you mention your children  they have certainly become part of your  storytelling so are we paying them yet  how's that going to work do they want to  be journalists good question are we  paying them no we're not but they do ask  sometimes  that started because you get stuck at  home and  schools around the country have you know  had been using our stories to teach  character education we we knew this so  that when the pandemic happened and kids  were stuck at home we started something  we called kindness 101  where we took stories and we sort of  framed them and used them to teach  lessons on on empathy or gratitude or  honesty integrity and  and it was pretty successful but we  found that it was meant for kids but it  was mostly adults who were watching this  thing so now cbs is putting that on the  broadcast on cbs morning so my kids are  now on broadcast television and i know  that they're going to be asking for an  increase in allowance because of it  you're anticipating it yes  i love that and i love that you're able  to showcase a little bit of your family  that becomes real you know the reality  of what it's like to work at home for so  many people who were stuck yeah it was  really because they would come in and  they're they want to tell me everything  and you know  you don't have to work from home very  often but for those who do they know  it's very hard because you know you  don't get more than like a few minutes  at a time before somebody's asking you  for something or and i feel so guilty  that can you come outside and play with  us i i feel like i have to say yes every  time  and do you i do  and you're a good dad yeah i guess so so  does that come first dad and then  journalist yeah i try i try to do it  that way but it you know it can be  stressful  i think every parent can probably  understand that  what is it that you think your stories  do for people the ones who are at home  who are watching  what do those stories give to them  what i'm trying to do through my work is  is remind people of what america is  really all about  and it's about people helping each other  people getting along whether they're  democrat or republican when you come  face to face with somebody you don't  care what their politics are you don't  care what their skin color is  people  like each other and i think we need to  be reminded of that and people are good  people are good  you know there are certain exceptions of  course but by and large people are good  and i just want to remind people of that  you know week in and week out  is that your goal for each story  it is it is to to reaffirm again restore  faith in humanity  and and present a true picture it's not  like i'm presenting just a  rosy image of america i feel like i'm  presenting the real picture of what  america is all about  i have to ask because we've the news  business has changed so much over the  years since i've been in it especially  since you've been in it  how is it that you've been able to  retain this feature  type  happy telling of stories you know the  the feel-good moments when the push is  for heart news hard and fast 20 seconds  a minute 20. how have you been able to  retain that  well it's funny because you know as the  news has gotten harsher let's put it  that way i have worried off and on that  maybe my days are coming to an end as a  reporter because i thought there'd be  more like  there isn't room for this kind of  feel-good thing that i do it turned out  to be just the opposite it turned out  that there was more of an appetite for  it than ever and so now i'm not worried  about losing my job at this point  because i do i do feel like there will  always be an appetite for it and the  worse the world gets the more appetite  there is for it  so it is important  i think it is important you know the way  we structure our news guests you know it  is a construct we put the worst story  that whatever the worst thing that  happened that goes first for no logical  reason nobody ever said that and then  the least important which is usually the  life-affirming thing comes at the end  you know i've talked to my bosses before  i don't switch it around and put and  they laugh at me but maybe someday  you just keep pushing keep pushing if  anyone can do it steve hartman can do it  i know it that's how it's going to work  i have to ask you and this and this is a  this is a tough question because you've  worked for so many years and done so  many incredible stories do you have one  that stands out is there one that is the  most memorable for you  yeah i am asked that sometimes and when  pressed i will say the story of nora and  dan  and that was a story about a little girl  who was in a grocery store and she saw  this grumpy old man and she asked him  for a hug  and what she didn't know what she would  have no way of knowing is that the man  had just lost his wife about six months  earlier and he'd given up his reason for  living  they hugged they became friends and they  were they saw each other every week and  gave him a renewed reason to live and i  like that story because it kind of  symbolizes all the stories  that we look for in that it was about as  insignificant little thing or seemingly  insignificant little thing that made a  huge impact and i think that's a theme  that you'll see through the the on the  road stories you know week in week week  out is something that seems so little  but it somehow just really makes a big  difference in sometimes one person's  life and sometimes many people's lives  and so that's it when forced to choose i  usually choose that one  beautiful story and and tough because  you've done so many you have something  that you're that you're talking about  for memorial day speaking of trying to  get everybody involved you've been doing  it a couple years talk about  your endeavor for taps okay well this  started  many years ago i did a story we'd gotten  an email saying i keep hearing taps i  live in tacoma washington i don't know  where it's coming from so my producer at  the time said well he started digging  down let's try to figure this out we  found a guy named don britton who was  standing on his porch  every day at sunset and he was playing  taps and we went to do a story on this  like what his motivation was  and  what surprised us most on that shoot was  that when he would play  other people would come out and stand on  their balconies stand on their porches  and they would stand their hand over  heart and it was a beautiful thing and i  thought what if all of america could  feel what i was feeling in that moment  and years went by and then the pandemic  happened and  there were no memorial day parades or  ceremonies so we said maybe this is the  year so we started taps across america  and basically what it is is people who  play trumpet or bugle we ask them to  stand on their porches or in their front  yards and play taps and if you don't you  know go out there and just take take a  moment  and we do it at three o'clock on  memorial day which is the moment of  remembrance and take a moment just to  reflect on what the holiday is all about  and we got a huge response and this is  gonna be the third year and we're we're  hoping this becomes like a national  tradition where you know long after i'm  gone this this this carries on music  does soothe the soul as i'm sure you  have discovered in so many of your  stories  is there anything that you want viewers  or other journalists to know about  yourself and about your path  um  that's funny because you know it's been  unusual having my kids you know in the  limelight with me and people being able  to see my family um because normally i  don't like to focus too much on on it  and i still don't want to because you  know as a journalist  we're the storyteller right and  sometimes we do need to be involved in  the story but at the same time the  it's about the people that we're  profiling and i wouldn't really  want any attention focused you know on  myself versus these incredible americans  that i get to meet and share their story  that's really what it's all about  always the humble storyteller thank you  thank you on the road right here in las  vegas we appreciate it thank you  excellent good stuff  okay in our audience anything else you  guys would like to need to ask oh that  was very good so uh so now what we'll do  is we'll roll the tape  that  [Laughter]  i know i'm sure their questions will be  different you know as  we have as you know newsrooms have  changed so much

